From: Vorapol Rinsatitnon <vorapol.r@pm.me>
Date: Fri, 04 Sep 2026 17:22:25 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] runtime: clobber X15 with SSE2 in the cgo callback test

The C half of TestCgoCallbackX15 (CL 770560, Go 1.27) clobbers X15 before
calling back into Go, to check that cgocallback restores the zero value that
ABIInternal requires. It does so with

	vmovdqu %0, %%xmm15

which is VEX encoded. A VEX instruction is an invalid opcode until the
operating system has enabled AVX, and Windows 7 does that only from Service
Pack 1.

	vmovdqu on Windows 7 RTM  ->  STATUS_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION
	                          ->  the program dies before the callback

Clobber the register with the SSE2 movdqu instead. It loads the same 128 bits
from the same address, differs only in encoding, and every amd64 processor has
SSE2.

This deviates from upstream, whose clobber is always VEX.

diff --git a/src/runtime/testdata/testprogcgo/callback_amd64.go b/src/runtime/testdata/testprogcgo/callback_amd64.go
index 5466745e..d0402e24 100644
--- a/src/runtime/testdata/testprogcgo/callback_amd64.go
+++ b/src/runtime/testdata/testprogcgo/callback_amd64.go
@@ -13,10 +13,13 @@ package main
 void go_callback_amd64();
 
 static void call_go_callback_amd64() {
-	// Clobber X15.
+	// Clobber X15. This is SSE2 rather than the VEX encoded vmovdqu the
+	// register invites, because VEX raises an invalid opcode unless the
+	// operating system enables AVX, and Windows 7 does that only from
+	// Service Pack 1 onwards.
 	uint64_t val = 42;
 	asm volatile(
-		"vmovdqu %0, %%xmm15;"
+		"movdqu %0, %%xmm15;"
 		:
 		: "m" (val)
 		: "xmm15");
